window.PORTFOLIO={"profile":{"name":"Prabhjot Bhandal","firstName":"Prabhjot","lastName":"Bhandal","program":"Mechatronics Engineering & Society","university":"McMaster University","level":"Third year","graduation":"2029 (expected)","educationStart":"2024","email":"bhandp9@mcmaster.ca","github":"https://github.com/Prabhjot-Bhandal","linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/prabhjot-bhandal/","resume":"assets/Prabhjot_Bhandal_Resume.pdf","portrait":"assets/portrait.jpg","tagline":"Break. Build. Innovate.","introduction":"I connect embedded software, electronics, and human-centred design. My projects focus on how the parts fit together, how a team moves the work forward, and who the system is for.","about":["As a kid, I was always taking things apart, from toys and pens to my family’s TV. The messes could be discouraging, but they came from a deep curiosity about how things worked. Rebuilding my first pen gave that curiosity a direction, long before I knew to call it engineering.","High school physics turned that habit into a love of solving problems I could connect to everyday life. I earned the highest Grade 12 physics mark at my school, became head of training for our DECA chapter, and helped lead our computer club as it organized ArrowHack, our school’s first hackathon.","That path brought me to McMaster University. I’m now a third-year Mechatronics Engineering & Society student, bringing together mechanical design, electronics, and software. The community, co-op opportunities, and chance to keep learning are part of why I chose McMaster.","I still believe a project only fails when I give up. Whether I’m debugging an embedded system, learning a new tool, or working through a problem with a team, I keep looking for the next fix. I sum up that mindset in three words. Break. Build. Innovate."],"toolkit":[{"title":"Programming","items":["C/C++","Python","Java","JavaScript","HTML/CSS"]},{"title":"Electronics & embedded systems","items":["ESP32","Arduino","I²C","KiCad","Altium simulation","Soldering"]},{"title":"Design & fabrication","items":["Autodesk Inventor","FDM 3D printing"]},{"title":"Delivery & automation","items":["Git","GitHub","Azure DevOps","Docker","Portainer","Copilot","Loop"]}],"how":[{"title":"Integrate","description":"Review the interfaces between physical parts, electronics, and software. On MAST, I review dimensions and connections across Avionics, Fuselage, Payload, and Landing Gear."},{"title":"Organize","description":"Break large tasks into steps a team can act on. On the drone team, I co-planned the electrical build and organized weekly syncs for eight members."},{"title":"Teach","description":"Make it easier to say “I’m stuck.” At coding camp, I taught Python and basic algorithms, developed a curriculum with other educators, and helped students complete their final projects."}],"experience":[{"dates":"JUN 2026 TO PRESENT","role":"Avionics Subteam Member","organization":"McMaster Aerospace Team","description":"Review dimensions and interfaces across four aircraft subsystems, raise integration concerns, and propose mitigations. Planned tests cover battery resistance and discharge rate.","tag":"SYSTEMS INTEGRATION"},{"dates":"MAY TO AUG 2026","role":"Student Project Coordinator","organization":"Equitable · Group Technology Modernization","description":"Coordinated four sprints, maintained the Azure DevOps backlog, and built Copilot and Loop workflows for meeting notes and leadership actions. Acted as project manager for nine days and trained the incoming coordinator.","tag":"TECHNICAL DELIVERY"},{"dates":"FEB TO JUN 2026","role":"Electrical Team Lead","organization":"McMaster Aerial Robotics & Drone Team","description":"Planned the electrical build with my co-lead, divided the work into tasks for eight members, and designed and simulated a buck converter in Altium.","tag":"ELECTRONICS"},{"dates":"SEPT 2025 TO PRESENT","role":"MES & External Liaison","organization":"McMaster Engineers with Disabilities","description":"Represent MEwD at MES meetings, make the case for funding and support, document action items, and report on resource use and progress.","tag":"COMMUNITY"},{"dates":"SUMMER 2024","role":"Student Tutor, Coding Camp","organization":"Peel District School Board","description":"Taught a group of 30 students in Grades 6 to 10 each day alongside two other educators. Co-developed a Python curriculum and delivered a cryptography escape-room workshop.","tag":"TEACHING"}],"footer":"Break. Build. Innovate.","aboutQuote":"Destruction is the birth of invention.","universityLogo":"assets/mcmaster-logo.png","beyond":"Outside engineering, I enjoy exploring cities and nature, learning local history and legends, and analysing the systems and meanings in media."},"projects":[{"id":"synabond","number":"01","title":"Synabond","subtitle":"A habit companion, built from circuit to firmware","category":"Embedded systems","categories":["Embedded systems","Electronics"],"period":"Winter 2026","scope":"Individual project · MECHTRON 2TA4","role":"Embedded systems designer & developer","tags":["C/C++","ESP32","KiCad","I²C"],"description":"An ESP32 habit tracker connects nine daily routines to a virtual pet, with RGB mood feedback and a reminder chosen from habits completed together.","summary":"I built Synabond to make a daily checklist feel more like looking after a companion. When you check off a habit on the breadboard device, the pet responds. At a scheduled time, the OLED can suggest an unfinished habit based on past routines.","cardOutcome":"10+ subsystem and edge-case tests · 2 hardware demos","outcome":"10+ tests","outcomeText":"I tested habit entry, checked the completion counts, and verified which habit the firmware chose for a nudge. The two hardware recordings show the interface and the seeded nudge test.","context":"Daily habit completion drives the pet’s mood, while long-term consistency supports progress and evolution. The device brings habit logging, timekeeping, visual feedback, and persistent data into a compact embedded system.","contribution":"I built the ESP32-based device and its C/C++ firmware, integrating the display, real-time clock, buttons, RGB feedback, and non-volatile habit and pet data. I also created the schematic in KiCad.","work":["Integrated an ESP32-WROOM-32, a 128 × 64 SSD1306 OLED, a DS3231 real-time clock, three pushbuttons, and an RGB LED. The OLED and RTC share an I²C bus.","Implemented separate finite-state machines for navigation and pet mood, with external interrupts for button presses and a timer interrupt for LED behaviour.","Built a Hebbian habit-weight matrix: +0.05 for co-completion, ×0.99 daily decay, and weights bounded between −1 and +1. Habit relationship scores determine a keystone habit to nudge.","Scheduled OLED and LED nudges, saved pet and habit data in non-volatile memory, and tested subsystem behaviour and habit-entry edge cases."],"architecture":"Button inputs feed the navigation and habit logic. The RTC keeps time for scheduled nudges. The OLED and RGB LED show the device’s state and give feedback. Non-volatile storage keeps the pet and habit data across restarts.","decision":"Separate state machines keep navigation and pet behaviour understandable. The firmware uses habit relationships to choose a suggested action, while today’s completion count determines mood. This keeps immediate feedback distinct from the longer-term habit model.","result":"The completed device combines habit logging, pet feedback, persistent progress, and personalised nudges. The test work included simulations to check that the device selected the intended habit for a nudge.","tools":["C/C++","ESP32-WROOM-32","SSD1306 OLED","DS3231 RTC","I²C","KiCad","Non-volatile storage","U8g2","RTClib","ESP32 Preferences","PWM"],"figureLabel":"Synabond architecture: buttons, real-time clock, and persistent data connect to the ESP32, with OLED and RGB LED feedback.","figureCaption":"Architecture illustration · components and connections from project notes","status":"Built & tested","note":"Individual embedded systems project. I designed the circuit, wrote the firmware, and documented the prototype for MECHTRON 2TA4.","order":1,"published":true,"diagram":"synabond","image":"","imageAlt":"","gallery":[],"links":[],"caseStudy":[{"id":"overview","label":"Overview","title":"One pet, two kinds of progress","paragraphs":["I wanted the pet to respond both to what I did today and to what I kept doing over time. Its mood and LED colour reflect today’s completed habits. At the daily reset, the pet earns experience toward its next level and evolution.","I designed the KiCad schematic, wired the ESP32 breadboard, and wrote the C/C++ firmware that connects the buttons, display, clock, and feedback. This is the infographic I made to explain the finished design."],"figures":[{"src":"assets/projects/synabond/infographic.png","source":"assets/projects/synabond/project-infographic.pdf","alt":"Original Synabond infographic with the hardware list, habit-weight update rules, circuit, and two state machines.","caption":"The one-page design overview from my final project."}]},{"id":"demos","label":"Demos","title":"Watch the prototype on the bench","paragraphs":["The first recording follows the daily logging interaction. For the second, I seeded the connection matrix with a known pattern so I could check which unfinished habit the firmware would suggest."],"videos":[{"title":"Regular demonstration","src":"assets/projects/synabond/regular-demo.mp4","poster":"assets/projects/synabond/regular-demo-poster.jpg","duration":"0:37","description":"I use the three buttons to navigate the habit log, check off entries, and return to the pet screen. Watch the OLED count and the RGB feedback change."},{"title":"Keystone-habit nudge test","src":"assets/projects/synabond/nudge-test.mp4","poster":"assets/projects/synabond/nudge-test-poster.jpg","duration":"0:26","description":"With the test pattern loaded, “Lunch Walk” appears beneath the pet’s status, and the RGB LED cycles through colours. The recording closes in on the OLED so the selected habit is visible."}]},{"id":"hardware","label":"Hardware","title":"A shared bus for the clock and display","paragraphs":["The ESP32-WROOM-32 runs a 128 × 64 SSD1306 OLED and a DS3231 real-time clock on the same I²C bus. The battery-backed clock supplies the time for reminders. The OLED has room for the pet sprite and its status on one screen and for a clock and scrolling checklist on the other."],"bullets":["The submitted sketch starts I²C on GPIO pins 21 and 22. U8g2 draws the interface, and RTClib reads the clock.","Three pushbuttons on GPIO pins 23, 19, and 18 handle up, select, and down or enter. The navigation module uses external interrupts and a 300 ms debounce interval.","The firmware drives the RGB channels on GPIO pins 25, 26, and 27. A hardware timer updates the PWM values, fading to the mood colour or cycling through colours during a nudge."],"figures":[{"src":"assets/projects/synabond/schematic.png","source":"assets/projects/synabond/circuit-schematic.pdf","alt":"KiCad schematic showing the ESP32, three pushbuttons, RGB LED with resistors, DS3231M clock, and SSD1306 OLED.","caption":"My KiCad schematic shows how the input, timekeeping, and feedback circuits connect."}]},{"id":"states","label":"State design","title":"Two screens and five pet moods","paragraphs":["The navigation state machine starts on the idle screen. Pressing the right button opens the log. The left and right buttons move the cursor, and the middle button toggles a habit or selects BACK. Returning to idle updates the total and the pet’s mood.","The pet has its own state mapping. That let me change the checklist navigation independently of the feedback for completing routines."],"table":{"caption":"Daily completion and pet feedback","columns":["Habits completed","Pet state","LED colour"],"rows":[["0–1","CRITICAL","Red"],["2–3","NEGLECTED","Orange"],["4–5","OKAY","Yellow"],["6–7","HAPPY","Green"],["8–9","THRIVING","Aqua"]]},"figures":[{"src":"assets/projects/synabond/navigation-fsm.png","source":"assets/projects/synabond/navigation-fsm.pdf","alt":"Navigation FSM from IDLE_STATE to LOG_STATE with the right button and back to idle when BACK is selected.","caption":"Original navigation state diagram."},{"src":"assets/projects/synabond/pet-fsm.png","source":"assets/projects/synabond/pet-fsm.pdf","alt":"Original pet-state table with daily completion thresholds and RGB output for each of the five moods.","caption":"My submitted pet-state table.","disclosure":"View the original pet-state table"}]},{"id":"firmware","label":"Firmware","title":"From a button press to a saved habit","paragraphs":["The Arduino sketch initializes the hardware, then calls the scheduler and the current navigation action in its main loop. I divided the implementation into seven C++ modules, each with a header file and an implementation file."],"items":[{"title":"Input and display","text":"navigation.cpp handles the buttons, cursor, scrolling, and screen transitions. ui.cpp draws the habit list, time, mood, and experience bar. sprites.cpp supplies the animation frames."},{"title":"Habit and pet state","text":"habits.cpp owns the checklist. pet.cpp maps the daily total to a mood and LED colour, manages experience and levels, and selects the evolution sprite."},{"title":"Time and reminders","text":"scheduler.cpp checks the RTC for the midnight reset and reminder times. hebbian.cpp updates connections between habits and selects a nudge candidate."},{"title":"Progress across power cycles","text":"The firmware uses ESP32 Preferences for non-volatile storage. It saves habit data, connection weights, pet level and experience, and the date of the last reset."}]},{"id":"model","label":"Habit model","title":"Why the demo suggests “Lunch Walk”","paragraphs":["I represented nine habits in a 9 × 9 matrix. At the daily update, the weight for each pair of habits completed together increases by 0.05. The firmware then multiplies each off-diagonal weight by 0.99 and bounds it between −1 and +1. Repeated co-completions can strengthen a connection, while an unused connection gradually weakens.","The intended selection rule sums the weights in each habit’s row and chooses an unfinished habit with the highest positive sum. In the submitted demo, I seeded “Lunch Walk” with three connections of 0.8, 0.7, and 0.6. That gives it a known score of 2.1 and an observable result to check on the OLED.","The regular scheduler checks for reminders at 10:00, 13:00, and 18:00. The submitted sketch enables the nudge demo so I could trigger it during a short recording. At midnight, the daily routine awards experience, updates the weights, clears completion flags, and saves progress.","A study by Phillippa Lally and her colleagues on repetition and habit formation informed my choice to model repeated behaviour over time. I chose the growth and decay constants for this prototype. The recordings document the device-level tests."]},{"id":"files","label":"Project files","title":"Drawings, firmware, recordings, and references","paragraphs":["These are the materials from the build and final submission. The PDFs and source ZIP are the original files. Both recordings are available in full with audio."],"resources":[{"src":"assets/projects/synabond/system-description.pdf","title":"System description","description":"My three-page account of the device, pet progression, and habit model.","format":"PDF","bytes":108142},{"src":"assets/projects/synabond/project-infographic.pdf","title":"Project infographic","description":"One page covering the components, design decisions, circuit, and states.","format":"PDF","bytes":328705},{"src":"assets/projects/synabond/circuit-schematic.pdf","title":"Circuit schematic","description":"Full-resolution KiCad drawing of the prototype circuit.","format":"PDF","bytes":71901},{"src":"assets/projects/synabond/navigation-fsm.pdf","title":"Navigation state machine","description":"The idle/log diagram and the button conditions for each transition.","format":"PDF","bytes":25797},{"src":"assets/projects/synabond/pet-fsm.pdf","title":"Pet-state table","description":"Daily completion thresholds and their RGB outputs.","format":"PDF","bytes":24066},{"src":"assets/projects/synabond/state-machines.pdf","title":"Combined state-machine document","description":"Both state-machine sheets in their original two-page submission.","format":"PDF","bytes":49044},{"src":"assets/projects/synabond/synabond-firmware.zip","title":"Complete firmware source","description":"The Arduino sketch and seven C++ module pairs, including sprites and the enabled nudge demo.","format":"ZIP","bytes":59446},{"src":"assets/projects/synabond/regular-demo.mp4","title":"Regular demonstration","description":"The full recording of habit logging and pet feedback on the breadboard.","format":"MP4","bytes":16754070},{"src":"assets/projects/synabond/nudge-test.mp4","title":"Nudge test","description":"The full recording of the seeded “Lunch Walk” nudge.","format":"MP4","bytes":16606791},{"src":"assets/projects/synabond/video-links.pdf","title":"Original video links","description":"The submitted sheet with the two YouTube links.","format":"PDF","bytes":20936},{"src":"assets/projects/synabond/habit-formation-research.pdf","title":"Habit-formation research","description":"How are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world. Lally, van Jaarsveld, Potts, and Wardle (2010).","format":"PDF","bytes":4346656}]}]},{"id":"streetsense","number":"02","title":"StreetSense","subtitle":"Crosswalk guidance through touch","category":"Accessible design","categories":["Embedded systems","Electronics"],"period":"February–April 2025","scope":"McMaster · ENGINEER 1P13 team project","role":"Embedded software contributor","tags":["Arduino","C/C++","IR sensing","ADXL345"],"description":"A white cane attachment that reads crosswalk markings and cane tilt, then signals a course correction through vibration.","summary":"I wrote the sensing and calibration firmware for a team-built white cane attachment. One or two pulses tell a deafblind user which way to steer.","cardOutcome":"One pulse to steer left. Two to steer right.","outcome":"Reported 40%","outcomeText":"Our team reported a 40% improvement in crosswalk alignment, based on staying within the markings and travelling straight.","context":"Crosswalk navigation depends on information that can be difficult to access without sight or hearing. The project explored how a cane attachment could give the user directional information through vibration.","contribution":"I developed C/C++ calibration and angle-processing logic that translated infrared and accelerometer readings into direction-coded vibration patterns. The calibration was refined through testing.","work":["Used an IR sensor to distinguish crosswalk markings from the road and an ADXL345 accelerometer to detect tilt-based veering.","Developed C/C++ calibration and angle-processing code that triggered vibration patterns tied to a direction.","Refined calibration through testing. The user recalibrates before entering a crosswalk and after turns.","Contributed to the team-built Arduino cane attachment and its reported improvement in crosswalk alignment."],"architecture":"Infrared and accelerometer measurements enter the Arduino. Calibration and filtering prepare the readings for control logic, which activates vibration motors to communicate guidance through touch.","decision":"The interface communicates through vibration so guidance does not depend on a visual display or an audible alert. Calibration and filtering support a more useful relationship between physical movement and tactile feedback.","result":"Contributed to a reported 40% improvement in crosswalk alignment accuracy, defined as staying in bounds and travelling straight. The baseline for this reported team result has not been established in the project material.","tools":["Arduino","C/C++","IR sensor","ADXL345 accelerometer","Vibration motors","Git","Soldering"],"figureLabel":"StreetSense cane attachment with its signal path from infrared contrast and tilt sensing to Arduino control and vibration.","figureCaption":"Signal-flow illustration · functional concept, not a hardware drawing","status":"Prototype","note":"Accessible navigation prototype developed for Power in Community at McMaster University.","order":2,"published":true,"diagram":"smart-cane","image":"","imageAlt":"","gallery":[],"links":[],"caseStudy":[{"id":"design","label":"Design brief","title":"Starting with the cane","paragraphs":["Our first concept used neon NFC labels to identify objects. During our second Q&A with the client, we learned that she already had a reliable way to organize her possessions. The colours she could distinguish also depended on the background. That conversation led us to reconsider where a device could help.","I sketched an attachment that could read the ground beneath her existing cane. Our team narrowed the task to crosswalk alignment, where the contrast between painted stripes and pavement gave us something the cane could sense."],"figures":[{"src":"assets/projects/streetsense/cane-concept.jpg","alt":"Hand-drawn ground identifier with a cylindrical cane mount, a light, and a colour sensor aimed at the ground.","caption":"My Milestone 2 concept sketch. The light and sensor face the ground, and the body fits around the cane."}]},{"id":"prototype","label":"Prototype","title":"From sidewalk joints to painted stripes","paragraphs":["My first Arduino prototype used a photoresistor as a stand-in for a colour or infrared sensor. An orange LED marked each dark sidewalk joint. A blue LED switched on after four seconds without another joint. I proposed counting missed joints and false alarms to compare sensor angles and timing settings.","The crosswalk design looks for bright painted stripes. A stripe resets the timer. After more than four seconds of low contrast, the controller checks the cane’s tilt to decide whether to issue a directional cue."],"figures":[{"src":"assets/projects/streetsense/breadboard.png","alt":"Arduino Uno connected to a breadboard with pushbuttons, potentiometers, LEDs, a transistor, and a vibration motor.","caption":"Milestone 4 circuit layout. The breadboard drawing shows the controls and motor output used to develop the guidance logic."}]},{"id":"firmware","label":"Firmware","title":"Turning sensor readings into a correction","paragraphs":["I developed the C/C++ calibration and angle-processing logic. IR calibration averages ten readings over a high-contrast surface to set the detection threshold. Accelerometer calibration records the starting orientation so the ADXL345 measures changes relative to the user’s starting cane angle.","When the stripe timer expires, the firmware compares the relative tilt against a threshold. The motor then signals which way to steer. A user can recalibrate the sensors after changing direction."],"table":{"caption":"Haptic cues after more than four seconds without a stripe","columns":["Detected motion","Motor cue","Correction"],"rows":[["Veering left","Two pulses","Steer right"],["Veering right","One pulse","Steer left"],["Travelling straight","No vibration","Continue straight"]]},"figures":[{"src":"assets/projects/streetsense/navigation-overview.png","alt":"Crosswalk sensing and feedback flowchart showing the colour indicator and three motion outcomes.","caption":"Presentation overview of the stripe detector and haptic cues."},{"src":"assets/projects/streetsense/navigation-flow.png","alt":"Portrait flowchart following the start button, stripe detection, time check, and directional feedback branches.","caption":"Presentation flowchart with the full decision path.","disclosure":"View the step-by-step navigation flowchart"},{"src":"assets/projects/streetsense/firmware-flow.png","alt":"Detailed firmware flowchart showing setup, calibration functions, the four-second timeout, relative-angle tests, and vibration decisions.","caption":"Report flowchart showing setup(), the sensor routines, and the logic that controls the motor.","disclosure":"View the detailed firmware flowchart"}]},{"id":"outcome","label":"Outcome","title":"Alignment and design lessons","paragraphs":["Our team reported a 40% improvement in crosswalk alignment, based on how well the user stayed within the markings and travelled straight. The available milestones document the prototype and proposed tests. They do not specify the baseline for that percentage.","This project changed how I test an idea. I now walk through the task with the user’s existing method in mind and draw the decision path early enough to catch assumptions before building."]},{"id":"documents","label":"Design files","title":"Original design documents","paragraphs":["The individual workbook gathers the design process in one place. The milestone submissions and reflection open separately below."],"resources":[{"src":"assets/projects/streetsense/design-workbook.pdf","title":"Individual design workbook","description":"Client notes, problem framing, concept exploration, prototype work, and reflection.","format":"PDF","bytes":2449460},{"src":"assets/projects/streetsense/concept-exploration.pdf","title":"Milestone 2 · Concepts","description":"Spatial-awareness research and sketches of two competing concepts.","format":"PDF","bytes":1132101},{"src":"assets/projects/streetsense/initial-prototype.pdf","title":"Milestone 3 · Prototype","description":"Arduino circuit, photoresistor logic, sample code, and the proposed test plan.","format":"PDF","bytes":497275},{"src":"assets/projects/streetsense/design-reflection.pdf","title":"Design reflection","description":"How client feedback changed my assumptions and how I would test the next design.","format":"PDF","bytes":303899}]}]},{"id":"drone-electronics","number":"03","title":"Drone Electronics","subtitle":"Power conversion & team integration","category":"Electronics","categories":["Electronics"],"period":"February–June 2026","scope":"McMaster Aerial Robotics & Drone Team","role":"Electrical Team Lead","tags":["Altium","Circuit design","Simulation"],"description":"I designed and simulated a buck converter to power 5 V logic from a 12S battery supply and helped coordinate the drone team’s electrical build.","summary":"Electrical design work for a student aerial robotics team, focused on low-voltage power conversion and coordination across the electrical group.","cardOutcome":"12S → 5 V converter design & simulation","outcome":"12S → 5 V","outcomeText":"I designed and simulated the buck converter in Altium for a 12S input and a 5 V logic output. PCB layout and fabrication were outside my scope.","context":"The team’s aircraft needed low-voltage logic power from a much higher-voltage battery supply. Power conversion and clear integration work were central to connecting the electrical subsystems.","contribution":"With a co-lead, I organized a four-month electrical build plan, split tasks into smaller modules, and ran weekly syncs for eight members. I also designed and simulated a buck converter in Altium.","work":["Designed and simulated a buck converter for a 12S source formed from two 6S batteries in series, targeting 5 V logic power. My scope did not include PCB layout or manufacturing.","Co-planned a four-month electrical build for eight members and split the work into small, modular tasks.","Discussed power delivery and integration between sensors, the microcontroller, and the mechanical body. Flagged high current draw and recommended thicker wire and shorter connections.","Documented PCB concepts and completed all but the logistics portion of the Altium Academy training."],"architecture":"Two 6S battery packs in series form the 12S input. The buck converter is designed to step the supply voltage down to 5 V for the logic circuits. The illustration shows the power relationship rather than a complete converter schematic.","decision":"The converter work focused on a clear boundary between the high-voltage battery supply and low-voltage logic power. Design notes and simulation supported review before any hardware fabrication.","result":"I designed and simulated the buck converter, documented PCB concepts, and coordinated the electrical team’s work. No measured outcome has been established for the four-month build plan.","tools":["Altium Designer","Circuit design","Circuit simulation","Altium Academy","Technical documentation"],"figureLabel":"Two 6S packs in series provide a 12S input to a buck converter designed to supply 5 V to the logic circuits.","figureCaption":"Power architecture illustration · design and simulation scope","status":"Design & simulation","note":"Student aerial robotics team project. My scope covered converter design, simulation, documentation, and coordination of the electrical team.","order":3,"published":true,"diagram":"drone-electronics","image":"","imageAlt":"","gallery":[],"links":[]},{"id":"skovel","number":"04","title":"The Skovel","subtitle":"A modular snow-travel multitool","category":"Mechanical design","categories":["Mechanical design"],"period":"2026 · 24-hour designathon","scope":"MDL Designathon · team of four engineering students","role":"Project Coordinator & CAD contributor","tags":["Inventor","FDM printing","Concept design"],"description":"A snow-travel concept combining foldable skis, extendable poles, and a shovel, developed through CAD, prototyping, and a team design pitch.","summary":"In a 24-hour design sprint, our team developed a modular concept for travelling on skis and digging with a shovel.","cardOutcome":"24-hour sprint · CAD, prototyping & design pitch","outcome":"24 hours","outcomeText":"Our team used the design sprint for concept development, CAD, FDM prototyping, documentation, and a presentation.","context":"The concept explored how a compact tool could support both travel through snow and digging. Foldable skis, extendable poles, magnetic attachments, and a heated-handle concept formed the proposed system.","contribution":"I coordinated deliverables and documentation, assigned work based on team members’ strengths, modelled the concept in Autodesk Inventor, and presented the design rationale to a mixed audience.","work":["Modelled the concept in Autodesk Inventor and contributed to the team’s FDM prototyping process.","Coordinated deliverables and delegated tasks based on team members’ strengths during the 24-hour sprint.","Helped subteams resolve design questions and supported documentation and the pitch deck.","Presented the concept, design rationale, and constraints in plain language to a mixed audience."],"architecture":"The concept combines foldable skis with extendable poles and a shovel configuration. This illustration simplifies the configurations. The original CAD model shows the folding and attachment mechanisms.","decision":"The team used a modular concept to combine travel and digging functions. Sketches, CAD, and a prototype made the design easier to communicate within the limited sprint time.","result":"During the sprint, our team modelled, prototyped, and presented the concept. The heated handles were a proposed feature. Performance in snow has yet to be verified.","tools":["Autodesk Inventor","Concept modelling","FDM 3D printing","Documentation","Presentation"],"figureLabel":"Skovel concept configurations: a pair of skis with poles and a shovel configuration. This is an explanatory illustration, not the original CAD.","figureCaption":"Configuration illustration · simplified from the documented concept","status":"Designathon concept","note":"Team concept and prototype. This illustration is not the original CAD model.","order":4,"published":true,"diagram":"skovel","image":"","imageAlt":"","gallery":[],"links":[]},{"id":"home-server","number":"05","title":"Home Server Automation","subtitle":"A second life for everyday hardware","category":"Automation","categories":["Automation"],"period":"Summer 2026 to present","scope":"Personal infrastructure project","role":"Server setup & automation","tags":["Python","Docker","Portainer"],"description":"A repurposed HP ProDesk runs seven automation pipelines for media, backups, photo search, and music discovery.","summary":"A personal server that brings media services, backups, and ML-assisted discovery together on repurposed hardware.","cardOutcome":"7 automation pipelines on repurposed hardware","outcome":"7 pipelines","outcomeText":"The seven pipelines handle media, backups, ML-assisted photo search, and music playlist generation on a repurposed HP ProDesk.","context":"I repurposed an HP ProDesk with an 8th-generation Intel Core i7, 16 GB of RAM, and a 256 GB SSD as a personal server for everyday services.","contribution":"I configured the server and Docker environment, edited Docker Compose and YAML configuration files, debugged failed services, and wrote or modified Python automation scripts. I also started a gaming backlog tracker, which remains in progress.","work":["Configured seven automation pipelines using Docker and Portainer on a repurposed HP ProDesk and added a homepage for service management.","Set up existing software for media streaming through Jellyfin and Navidrome, photo and file backup through Immich, and network-wide ad blocking through Pi-hole.","Configured open-source ML models for photo search and music analysis to support playlists built from music I own.","Edited Docker Compose and YAML configurations, debugged service failures, and wrote or modified Python automation scripts.","Began a gaming backlog tracker in Python, JavaScript, HTML, and CSS, using the server as its database and host. This custom application is in progress."],"architecture":"Repurposed hardware hosts a Docker environment managed through Portainer. Services support media, backup, and discovery workflows, while a homepage provides a common entry point.","decision":"Reusing an existing desktop created a practical platform for running and managing services together. Containers provided the deployment structure, with Portainer and the homepage supporting everyday management.","result":"Configured seven automation pipelines across personal media, backup, and discovery workflows, bringing everyday services together on repurposed hardware.","tools":["Python","Docker","Docker Compose","YAML","Portainer","Jellyfin","Navidrome","Immich","Pi-hole"],"figureLabel":"Home server service map: HP ProDesk hardware runs Docker and Portainer, supporting media, backups, and ML-assisted discovery services.","figureCaption":"Service architecture illustration · not a live system dashboard","status":"Configured system","note":"Personal project using repurposed hardware and existing open-source services.","order":5,"published":true,"diagram":"home-server","image":"","imageAlt":"","gallery":[],"links":[],"wideCard":true},{"id":"game-launcher","order":6,"era":"earlier","title":"Custom Game Launcher","subtitle":"All your games in one place · Java desktop application","category":"Software","categories":["Software","Earlier work"],"period":"Pre-university · Grade 12 computer science","scope":"Individual programming project","role":"Java application developer","tags":["Java","Java Swing","Git"],"description":"A Java Swing library that discovers local game assets, builds detail panels, and opens each game’s configured webpage.","summary":"I built a desktop interface to bring games scattered across different platforms into one browsable library, with file-driven content and event-based navigation.","cardOutcome":"Asset-driven panels and webpage launching","outcome":"File-driven UI","outcomeText":"When I add game assets that follow the naming convention, the launcher creates a panel for them automatically. Launch actions open configured webpages.","context":"Games scattered across platforms were easy to forget or buy twice. I wanted one interface for browsing my library and opening the relevant game page.","contribution":"I developed the application individually in Java, including the Swing interface, asset loading, navigation, and event handling.","work":["Built dynamic game panels using JPanel and ImageIcon and used CardLayout to show one game’s details at a time.","Scanned a local assets folder using Java file I/O. The launcher adds files that follow the naming convention and ignores unrelated filenames.","Used action listeners to open the selected game’s webpage.","Used BorderLayout and GridBagLayout to accommodate different window sizes and tracked the project with Git."],"architecture":"Local image and description files feed the game list and detail cards. Selecting a game changes the visible CardLayout panel. The launch action opens that game’s configured webpage.","decision":"A predictable file convention kept new entries simple to add. CardLayout separated navigation from the content of each game panel.","result":"Produced a working desktop library interface with dynamic asset loading, game selection, and webpage launching. Direct executable launching, web scraping, and cloud sync were future ideas.","tools":["Java","Java Swing","JPanel","ImageIcon","CardLayout","BorderLayout","GridBagLayout","Git","File I/O"],"blocks":["Local assets","Swing library","Game webpage"],"status":"Earlier software project","note":"Individual Java project. Demonstrated features include local asset detection, Swing navigation, webpage launching, and adaptable window layouts.","image":"","imageAlt":"","imageCaption":"","gallery":[{"src":"assets/projects/game-launcher-2.png","alt":"Original screenshot of a game detail card with artwork, title, and description.","caption":"The original CardLayout detail view."}],"links":[{"label":"Source code","url":"https://github.com/Prabhjot-Bhandal/Java-Project-START/tree/b488c8e45b720fb015f34f3157e8dede11349444/Java%20Experiments/Grade%2012%20CS/GameLauncherREBORN"}],"published":true,"diagram":"game-launcher","figureLabel":"Illustrated Java Swing game library with local assets, selected game cards, and webpage launching.","figureCaption":"File-driven game library · explanatory illustration","caseStudy":[{"id":"problem","label":"Problem","title":"A library scattered across launchers","paragraphs":["Owning games on several platforms made it easy to forget what I already had or overlook something I wanted to play. I wanted one place to browse those titles and reach the relevant page.","I developed the application individually in Java. I wanted navigation to be straightforward, the whole library to be accessible from one interface, and new games to be easy to add."],"figures":[{"src":"assets/projects/game-launcher-1.png","alt":"Original screenshot of the Java Swing game library, with game navigation and a selected game panel.","caption":"Original application screenshot"}]},{"id":"assets","label":"Asset pipeline","title":"Let the files populate the interface","paragraphs":["The launcher uses Java file I/O to scan its local assets folder. It creates library entries from image and description files that follow the naming convention and ignores unrelated filenames.","JPanel and ImageIcon provide each game’s display content. Keeping game-specific assets outside the layout code let me add games without building each new panel by hand."],"figures":[{"src":"assets/projects/launcher-assets-demo.gif","alt":"Animation demonstrating the launcher’s local game-asset folder and naming convention.","caption":"Original demonstration of how game files are organized.","motion":true,"demoLabel":"Play asset-folder demonstration"}]},{"id":"navigation","label":"Navigation","title":"One selected game, one detail card","paragraphs":["The side panel provides access to the library. Selecting a title changes the visible CardLayout card, showing its artwork, name, and description.","Only one detail card is visible at a time. That keeps the main area focused on the selected game while the library remains available beside it."],"figures":[{"src":"assets/projects/game-launcher-2.png","alt":"Screenshot of a selected game detail panel with its artwork, title, and description.","caption":"The selected game’s CardLayout detail view."}]},{"id":"interaction","label":"Interaction","title":"Respond to actions and window changes","paragraphs":["An action listener responds when the user selects the launch option. It opens the webpage configured for the selected game.","BorderLayout and GridBagLayout manage the interface as the window changes size. I also used Git to track the application’s development."],"figures":[{"src":"assets/projects/launcher-webpage-demo.gif","alt":"Animation of the application opening the selected game’s webpage.","caption":"Original webpage-launching demonstration.","motion":true,"demoLabel":"Play webpage-launching demonstration"}]},{"id":"result","label":"Result","title":"A working, expandable desktop library","paragraphs":["The application brought file-based content loading, game selection, and webpage launching into one Swing interface. It gave me experience connecting Java file operations, GUI components, layout managers, and event handling around a problem I cared about.","The project showed me why it helps to keep frequently updated content separate from the interface that displays it."]},{"id":"extensions","label":"Next steps","title":"What I proposed beyond this version","paragraphs":["I would like to fetch cover art automatically, launch executables directly, support more operating systems, and synchronize libraries across devices. The version I built loads local assets and opens the webpage configured for each game."]}],"number":"06"},{"id":"clean-up-crab","order":7,"era":"earlier","title":"The Clean-Up Crab","subtitle":"Disaster-relief robotics · sensing and gripper design","category":"Embedded systems","categories":["Embedded systems","Electronics","Earlier work"],"period":"2023 · Grade 11 robotics","scope":"Individual classroom robotics project","role":"Robot designer & programmer","tags":["Arduino","C/C++","Tinkercad"],"description":"An Arduino debris-collection prototype combining distance and contact sensing with a redesigned four-bar gripper.","summary":"An individual robotics project that connected Arduino control, circuit prototyping, and a servo-driven gripper around a disaster clean-up concept.","cardOutcome":"Sensor-driven routines + redesigned four-bar gripper","outcome":"Sense → act","outcomeText":"I integrated ultrasonic and contact inputs with wheel movement, status LEDs, and a servo gripper. I revised the linkage to add a stationary mounting element.","context":"The classroom brief explored autonomous light-debris collection with a limited set of sensors. I developed the project individually, from sketches and circuit concepts through programming and troubleshooting.","contribution":"I researched sensors and gripper mechanisms, made orthographic drawings, prototyped the circuit, programmed the Arduino, and revised the physical gripper.","work":["Used an ultrasonic sensor for distance measurement and a limit switch for contact feedback.","Prototyped the circuit in Tinkercad and built a physical circuit with the Arduino, sensors, DC motors, LEDs, and a servo.","Programmed approach, obstacle-checking, turning, gripping, and release routines in C/C++.","Reworked a cardboard-and-dowel gripper into a four-bar crank-rocker arrangement with a stationary mounting element and round-head fastener joints.","Used the Serial Monitor and iterative prototyping to work through power, circuit, and programming issues."],"architecture":"Ultrasonic distance and contact feedback enter the Arduino control logic. The program drives the wheels and servo gripper, with LEDs used for status feedback.","decision":"The initial gripper lacked a stationary attachment point. Revising the linkage and simplifying the cardboard sides addressed mounting and rigidity problems.","result":"Created circuit and physical prototypes, control code, working drawings, and multiple gripper iterations. The work demonstrates an early classroom design process with sensor and actuator integration.","tools":["Arduino","C/C++","Ultrasonic sensor","Limit switch","DC motors","Servo motor","Tinkercad","Breadboard","Orthographic drawing"],"blocks":["Distance + contact","Arduino control","Motion + gripper"],"status":"Earlier robotics project","note":"Individual Grade 11 robotics project, documented in 2023. I built the classroom prototype to explore a possible application in disaster relief.","image":"","imageAlt":"","imageCaption":"","gallery":[{"src":"assets/projects/clean-up-crab-2.png","alt":"Original photograph of the revised cardboard four-bar gripper in its open position.","caption":"The revised gripper with a stationary mounting element."},{"src":"assets/projects/clean-up-crab-3.png","alt":"Original Tinkercad circuit screenshot with an Arduino, breadboard, motors, servo, and ultrasonic sensor.","caption":"The original Tinkercad circuit prototype."}],"links":[],"published":true,"diagram":"clean-up-crab","figureLabel":"Illustrated Arduino rover with ultrasonic sensing, wheel motors, and a servo-driven four-bar gripper.","figureCaption":"Sensing + motion + gripper · explanatory illustration","caseStudy":[{"id":"brief","label":"Brief","title":"A robot for lightweight debris","paragraphs":["The Clean-Up Crab began as a concept for collecting light debris after a natural disaster. The classroom prototype explored how a small mobile robot could detect an object, approach it, grip it, and release it elsewhere.","I owned the project from research and drawings through circuit construction, programming, and troubleshooting. The design had to be lightweight, use a simple gripper, and work with the instructor-provided sensors."],"figures":[{"src":"assets/projects/crab-system-sketch.png","alt":"My dimensioned isometric sketch of the proposed Clean-Up Crab body, wheels, sensors, and front gripper.","caption":"System sketch: the intended arrangement of the robot’s mechanical and electrical components."}]},{"id":"electronics","label":"Electronics","title":"From Tinkercad to a physical circuit","paragraphs":["I prototyped the circuit in Tinkercad before wiring up the Arduino, ultrasonic sensor, limit switch, DC motors, LEDs, and servo. The ultrasonic sensor measured distance, and the limit switch supplied contact feedback for the obstacle check.","Motor and sensor power issues required debugging across the circuit and control code. I used Serial Monitor output to inspect sensor readings and see which routine the program was executing."],"figures":[{"src":"assets/projects/clean-up-crab-3.png","alt":"Tinkercad circuit with an Arduino, breadboard, drive motors, servo, and ultrasonic sensor.","caption":"The circuit prototype in Tinkercad."},{"src":"assets/projects/clean-up-crab-1.png","alt":"Photograph of my physical Arduino circuit, drive motors, wiring, and ultrasonic sensor.","caption":"The physical circuit and component prototype."}]},{"id":"control","label":"Control logic","title":"Reactive decisions from distance and contact","paragraphs":["The program split behaviour into distance measurement, wall checking, grabbing, path changes, and individual movement routines. LEDs and serial messages made those behaviours visible while debugging.","This was a sensor-and-timing approach: the robot compared local distances and used timed turns and travel. It did not use a map or a localization system."],"items":[{"title":"Detect and check","text":"An object within 10 cm triggered wallCheck(). Contact feedback from the limit switch was used to distinguish an obstruction from an object the robot could attempt to move."},{"title":"Choose a direction","text":"For an obstruction, changePath() sampled right and left distances. The program chose the side with more clearance or turned around when both readings were equal."},{"title":"Grip and release","text":"When the measured distance fell below 5 cm, the grab routine moved the servo to 100° and turned the robot. After the robot travelled for a set time, the routine returned the servo to 0° to release the object."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/projects/crab-algorithm.jpg","alt":"Original flowchart showing robot movement, object checking, gripping, and release decisions.","caption":"My algorithm for detecting an object, checking for a wall, and deciding when to grip or release."}],"resources":[{"src":"assets/projects/clean-up-crab-sketch.zip","title":"Clean-Up Crab Arduino sketch","description":"My complete Arduino program, including ultrasonic ranging, limit-switch checks, motor control, and the servo gripper.","format":"ZIP","bytes":2807}]},{"id":"gripper","label":"Gripper design","title":"Adding the fixed link the mechanism needed","paragraphs":["My first cardboard-and-dowel gripper used a servo-driven metal rod to open and close its jaws. However, its moving joints left no stationary element that could be secured to the robot’s base.","I revised it into a four-bar crank-rocker arrangement. Round-head fasteners strengthened the joints, while a stationary link provided the mounting point. I also simplified the sides to single cardboard pieces for greater rigidity, retaining the paper-clip connection that drove the metal rod."],"figures":[{"src":"assets/projects/crab-gripper-initial.png","alt":"First cardboard gripper prototype in the open position, with wooden dowels at its moving joints.","caption":"Initial gripper: no suitable fixed element for mounting."},{"src":"assets/projects/clean-up-crab-2.png","alt":"Revised gripper open, with round-head fasteners and a fixed mounting element.","caption":"Revised four-bar gripper in the open position."},{"src":"assets/projects/crab-gripper-closed.png","alt":"Revised cardboard gripper in its closed position.","caption":"Revised gripper in the closed position."},{"src":"assets/projects/crab-working-drawings.png","alt":"Original dimensioned working drawings for the Clean-Up Crab.","caption":"Working drawings used to plan component placement and mechanical geometry."}]},{"id":"outcome","label":"Outcome","title":"Learning across the hardware–software boundary","paragraphs":["The project produced system sketches, working drawings, a circuit simulation, a physical circuit, Arduino control code, and successive gripper prototypes. It gave me practical experience tracing a problem across power, sensing, code, and mechanics.","The main mechanical lesson was to define how a moving assembly attaches to the rest of the system. The gripper needed both freedom to move and a stable point where it could attach to the robot."]},{"id":"future","label":"Future work","title":"Richer sensing and manipulation","paragraphs":["My original next-step ideas included colour or thermal sensors, illuminated camera sensing, and a six-degree-of-freedom arm for more flexible manipulation. These ideas were proposals for a future version."]}],"number":"07"},{"id":"arrowhack","order":8,"era":"earlier","title":"The ArrowHack","subtitle":"Building Fletcher’s Meadow’s first hackathon","category":"Leadership","categories":["Leadership","Earlier work"],"period":"December 12, 2023","scope":"Fletcher’s Meadow Secondary School · Brampton","role":"Director of Coordination","tags":["Event design","Workshops","Coordination"],"description":"I led coordination for our school’s first hackathon, where 60+ competitors built productivity apps and junior students could try robotics and programming.","summary":"As Director of Coordination, I helped bring a hackathon to Fletcher’s Meadow Secondary School in Brampton. More than 60 competitors took part in our first event. We also offered workshops for students who wanted to try robotics or practise programming.","cardOutcome":"60+ competitors · inaugural school hackathon","outcome":"60+ competitors","outcomeText":"Teams of two or three competed in a productivity-themed challenge. Other students could take part in robotics and Java workshops.","context":"Travel to hackathons in other cities made participation difficult. I wanted younger programmers to have a local opportunity to compete and learn, including options for those who preferred workshops.","contribution":"As Director of Coordination, I organized logistics, resources, scheduling, and executive-team coordination for the school’s first ArrowHack.","work":["Worked with school administration on funding, prizes, lunch, equipment, and a library venue.","Sourced Chromebooks to give competitors a consistent hardware starting point.","Coordinated the executive team, workshop preparation, event schedule, and branding materials.","Structured a productivity-themed competition for teams of two or three and included robotics and Java workshops.","Prepared judging criteria covering completeness, functionality and design, creativity, presentation, and effectiveness."],"architecture":"The event brought together a common hardware setup, a competition schedule, parallel learning opportunities, and a judging process. Coordination connected the venue, resources, workshops, participants, and judges.","decision":"Workshops gave participants a way to learn without entering the competition. Common devices and a shared rubric supported consistent expectations.","result":"The inaugural event brought together more than 60 competitors. After I graduated, ArrowHack continued at Fletcher’s Meadow and grew in subsequent editions.","tools":["Event organization","Project management","Scheduling","Workshop development","Adobe Photoshop","Canva","Java","Python","Robotics"],"blocks":["Resources + venue","Competition + workshops","Judging + recognition"],"status":"Earlier leadership project","note":"I led coordination with the computer club’s executive team, school administration, teacher sponsor, workshop organizers, and judges from the school and industry.","image":"","imageAlt":"","imageCaption":"","gallery":[],"links":[],"published":true,"diagram":"arrowhack","figureLabel":"Illustrated ArrowHack event with 60-plus competitors, shared laptops, and Java and robotics workshops.","figureCaption":"Competition + workshops · explanatory illustration","caseStudy":[{"id":"inspiration","label":"Inspiration","title":"Why I brought a hackathon to Brampton","paragraphs":["When I started programming, I wanted to enter hackathons to sharpen my skills and find out what I could build under pressure. I lived in Brampton, and the difficulty of getting to events in downtown Toronto or Waterloo kept me from taking part. I wanted junior programmers at Fletcher’s Meadow to be able to walk into their own school and have that experience.","We designed two ways to spend the day. Students could form a team and compete or attend robotics and Java workshops to build their skills. That gave a beginner a reason to come even if they had never built an app."],"figures":[{"src":"assets/projects/arrowhack-1.jpg","alt":"The ArrowHack executive team addressing students in the school library.","caption":"The executive team and I welcomed students to the first ArrowHack on December 12, 2023."}]},{"id":"coordination","label":"My role","title":"Organizing the people, equipment, and space","paragraphs":["As Director of Coordination, I worked with school administration to fund prizes, lunches, and equipment. I booked the library for the day and secured Chromebooks for every competitor. Providing the same hardware meant a student’s choice of personal laptop would have no bearing on the competition.","I guided the computer club’s executive team through workshop setup, the schedule, and design work on the logo and certificates. We used Adobe Photoshop and Canva for the graphics. I coordinated supervision and judging with people from the school and industry and planned lunch so the competitors and workshop groups could eat together."]},{"id":"schedule","label":"Event schedule","title":"One school day, two ways to participate","paragraphs":["I drew up a schedule that let the main competition run alongside activities for Grades 9 and 10. In the morning, the younger students had a VEX robot workshop and showcase in Room 108 and an ArrowHack Mini in the small room. We reunited the groups for lunch, judging, and awards."],"table":{"caption":"The schedule posted in the library","columns":["Time","Activities"],"rows":[["8:50–9:00 a.m.","Attendance and check-in."],["9:05–9:15 a.m.","Introductions and welcome."],["9:15–11:30 a.m.","ArrowHack Main in the big room. VEX workshop, showcase, and ArrowHack Mini for Grades 9 and 10."],["11:30 a.m.–12:25 p.m.","Lunch together."],["12:25–12:35 p.m.","Afternoon attendance and check-in."],["12:35–2:00 p.m.","ArrowHack Main resumed. The afternoon workshop appears on the schedule as “Workshop in JS.”"],["2:00–2:50 p.m.","Judging and gallery walk."],["2:50–3:10 p.m.","Awards and closing."]]},"figures":[{"src":"assets/projects/arrowhack-schedule.jpg","alt":"Library whiteboard with parallel columns for competitors and Grades 9 and 10, and the ArrowHack logo.","caption":"The whiteboard schedule I prepared for the main competition and the junior activities."}]},{"id":"challenge","label":"Challenge & rules","title":"Build a productivity tool in a team of two or three","paragraphs":["I structured the challenge around productivity. Teams chose a task they wanted to improve and then built an app or another software solution during the event. They could discuss ideas with one another and ask organizers or judges for advice. Each team had to write its own code on site that day."],"table":{"caption":"Competition rules and penalties","columns":["Rule","How we applied it"],"rows":[["Original code","Teams could research how a process worked. Copying and pasting someone else’s code and claiming it as their own meant immediate disqualification."],["AI assistance","Using ChatGPT or another AI-assisted tool meant immediate disqualification."],["Working with other teams","Discussing ideas was welcome. Directly assisting another team with its work or sabotaging it carried a 25-point deduction. Advice from organizers and judges was allowed."]]},"figures":[{"src":"assets/projects/arrowhack-mentoring.png","alt":"Prabhjot advising competitors at a library table.","caption":"I gave this team advice on how to present its solution to the judges."}]},{"id":"judging","label":"Judging","title":"What the judges looked for","paragraphs":["During the final hour, teams presented their projects to a panel of judges from the school and industry. The score sheet gave judges space to record strengths and weaknesses for each criterion. Each was worth 20 points, for a total of 100."],"table":{"caption":"ArrowHack scoring rubric","columns":["Criterion","What earned credit","Points"],"rows":[["Completeness","Evidence of a software product, whether a working prototype in progress or a finished application.","20"],["Functionality and design","Critical thinking about how the product should work and a design that supported those choices.","20"],["Creativity and innovation","An original approach to the problem and a solution with a distinctive idea behind it.","20"],["Presentation","A clear, effective, and enthusiastic explanation of what the product did and how it worked.","20"],["Effectiveness","A demonstration that the product worked as described and performed its intended task well.","20"]]},"figures":[{"src":"assets/projects/arrowhack-2.png","alt":"Blank judging sheet with rows for strengths, weaknesses, and scores across all five criteria.","caption":"The 100-point scoring sheet we gave the judges.","disclosure":"View the judging sheet"}]},{"id":"awards","label":"Awards","title":"Recognizing teams and individual strengths","paragraphs":["The first-place team received a cash prize. The top three teams received medals and Certificates of Distinction. Every participant received a Certificate of Participation.","Judges also recognized individuals who stood out in one of the five scoring categories during the competition. Those participants received Rubik’s Cubes, so a strong idea, design, or presentation could earn recognition beyond the overall team standings."],"figures":[{"src":"assets/projects/arrowhack-participation.png","alt":"Teal Certificate of Participation bearing the ArrowHack logo and inaugural event date.","caption":"The participation certificate given to everyone who took part."},{"src":"assets/projects/arrowhack-winning-certificate.png","alt":"Gold Certificate of Distinction for outstanding work at the ArrowHack.","caption":"The distinction certificate for members of the top three teams."},{"src":"assets/projects/arrowhack-medals.png","alt":"First-, second-, and third-place ArrowHack medals on blue ribbons.","caption":"The medals awarded to the three highest-scoring teams."}]},{"id":"impact","label":"Impact & skills","title":"What carried on after the first event","paragraphs":["More than 60 competitors came together for the inaugural ArrowHack. The shared equipment made it possible to enter without bringing a laptop, while the workshops gave newer programmers a way to take part before they felt ready to compete. After I graduated and began my engineering degree, the school continued to run ArrowHack, and the event grew in subsequent editions.","The coordination role drew on my leadership, teamwork, communication, and problem-solving skills. Writing the day’s schedule and securing the equipment gave me practice in time and project management. Designing for different experience levels made inclusivity a practical part of organizing an event. The project also drew on workshop development, graphic design, Java, Python, and robotics."]}],"number":"08"},{"id":"airport","order":9,"era":"earlier","title":"Automated Check-In & Luggage System","subtitle":"Airport innovation · software and mechanical integration","category":"Systems design","categories":["Mechanical design","Software","Earlier work"],"period":"First-year course project","scope":"Team project · ENGINEER 1P13","role":"Subject-matter expert · mechanism concept · Python oversold function","tags":["Python","Mechanism design","Research"],"description":"Python check-in software and an actuated luggage bridge, developed through three mechanical iterations.","summary":"Our team connected automated passenger check-in with luggage transfer. Repeated prototype failures taught me about torque, stiffness, and how to plan tests.","cardOutcome":"Python integration + three bridge iterations","outcome":"3 iterations","outcomeText":"A rotary bridge evolved into a linear-actuator design. The software functions worked together. Mount deflection prevented the final bridge from fully retracting.","context":"The team had to connect ticket verification and luggage handling with a transfer mechanism that could retract behind a restriction line. The work combined software decisions with actuator, geometry, and fabrication constraints.","contribution":"I gathered and assessed research as the team’s subject-matter expert, designed the retractable-bridge concept, and developed the oversold-seat function for the Python program.","work":["Built a research knowledge base covering programming resources, mechanisms, materials, and Inventor modelling concepts.","Developed the oversold function to determine excess business and economy bookings from fleet and passenger data.","Proposed two versions of a retractable bridge, one with a rotary actuator and one with a linear actuator. The team initially selected the rotary version.","Contributed to a team design process that revised gear thickness, bridge segmentation, and actuator choice in response to printing and testing constraints.","Reflected on torque, rod stiffness and slip, actuator-mount deflection, print scheduling, and the need for an error log."],"architecture":"The software side checked passenger and flight data, including oversold seats. The physical side explored an actuated bridge for transferring luggage between elevations. The project required those software and mechanical tasks to fit a shared system brief.","decision":"The initial rotary design was selected for its simplicity and low part count. Torque and attachment problems later led the team to reuse available parts with a linear actuator, exposing the importance of checking loads and stiffness early.","result":"The team integrated its check-in functions and resolved graphical formatting issues. The final mechanical design did not fully retract behind the required line because its mount deflected. My reflection identified earlier calculations, a reusable error log, and print planning as concrete improvements.","tools":["Python","Autodesk Inventor","Mechanism design","Research documentation","Team prototyping"],"blocks":["Passenger data","Check-in logic","Luggage-transfer concept"],"status":"Earlier course project","note":"ENGINEER 1P13 team project. My individual work included research, the original bridge concept, and the oversold-seat function. Our team revised the prototypes and integrated the software together.","image":"","imageAlt":"","imageCaption":"","gallery":[{"src":"assets/projects/airport-1.png","alt":"Original flowchart for the oversold-seat function in the passenger check-in program.","caption":"My original design for the Python oversold-seat function."}],"links":[],"published":true,"diagram":"airport","figureLabel":"Illustrated luggage-transfer bridge and linear actuator, with a Python check-in logic panel.","figureCaption":"Luggage-transfer concept · explanatory illustration","caseStudy":[{"id":"brief","label":"Brief","title":"One system, two engineering problems","paragraphs":["The brief combined a Python ticket-verification program with a mechanism to move luggage between two plates at different elevations. The mechanism had to retract behind a restriction line, fit the available fabrication process, and remain simple enough to revise.","The wider system also called for luggage classification, barcode-based acceptance or rejection, Q-Arm handling, and a Turtle Graphics summary of flight and passenger data. Our team divided the programming work and compared the mechanical concepts we had developed individually."],"bullets":["Mechanical constraints: actuator choice, retracted clearance, printer build area, and available print time.","Software constraints: readable, well-documented functions using techniques learned in class, so the team could integrate them."]},{"id":"contribution","label":"My role","title":"Research that supported design decisions","paragraphs":["As the subject-matter expert, I gathered and assessed sources and built a shared research database. It covered bridging structures, printing materials, mechanisms, Inventor techniques such as spur-gear modelling, and Python data structures.","I also designed the retractable-bridge concept and developed the oversold function. The team chose my rotary-actuator concept for its simplicity and low part count, then developed and tested the CAD prototypes together."],"figures":[{"src":"assets/projects/airport-2.png","alt":"My dimensioned sketch of the rotary-actuator retractable bridge.","caption":"My initial rotary-actuator concept."},{"src":"assets/projects/airport-linear-sketch.png","alt":"My dimensioned alternative bridge sketch using a linear actuator.","caption":"The linear-actuator alternative I explored at the concept stage."}]},{"id":"software","label":"Software","title":"Handling oversold seats in Python","paragraphs":["My function used the supplied fleet and passenger data to calculate how many business- and economy-class seats were overbooked on each plane. I planned its logic in a flowchart before combining it with the team’s other check-in functions.","During testing, we corrected minor syntax and formatting errors so the functions ran together. The graphical summary took more work. We adjusted the text alignment and resized the boxes."],"figures":[{"src":"assets/projects/airport-1.png","alt":"Flowchart for calculating oversold business and economy seats from fleet and passenger data.","caption":"My original oversold-function flowchart."}]},{"id":"iterations","label":"Prototypes","title":"Three iterations of the luggage bridge","paragraphs":[],"items":[{"title":"Rotary drive: simple concept, difficult fabrication","text":"The first CAD assembly used two spur gears: the actuator drove the smaller gear, while the larger gear rotated the bridge through an extended rod. Side mounts supported the rods, and bridge-edge ridges helped contain luggage.","observation":"The gears were unnecessarily thick, and the bridge exceeded the printer’s build area. Both details made the design harder to fabricate within the schedule.","figure":{"src":"assets/projects/airport-prototype-1.png","alt":"First team CAD prototype, showing the geared rotary drive and luggage bridge.","caption":"First team prototype: a rotary actuator and two spur gears."}},{"title":"Split bridge: printable geometry, unresolved loads","text":"The second version used thinner gears and divided the bridge into two slotted sections that could be glued together. Some parts still exceeded the in-class print window and needed external printing.","observation":"Testing exposed a flexible extended rod, slip at its circular coupling, and insufficient actuator torque to rotate the bridge.","figure":{"src":"assets/projects/airport-prototype-2.png","alt":"Second team CAD prototype with a revised gear and bridge assembly.","caption":"Second team prototype: thinner gears and a segmented bridge."}},{"title":"Linear drive: a new load path","text":"With one testing session left, the team reused printed parts and changed to a linear actuator to raise and lower the bridge.","observation":"The actuator mount bent under its own weight and the lifted load. This deflection kept the bridge from fully retracting behind the required line.","figure":{"src":"assets/projects/airport-prototype-final.png","alt":"Final team CAD prototype using a linear actuator to raise the bridge.","caption":"Final team prototype: the linear-actuator design."}}]},{"id":"decisions","label":"Tradeoffs","title":"A geometric fix did not solve a load problem","paragraphs":["Splitting the bridge solved the printer-envelope problem, but it did not address torque transmission or rod stiffness. The later actuator change moved the critical load into a mount that was too flexible.","Possible revisions included a thicker rod with a squared coupling, a different gear arrangement, and a stronger linear-actuator mount using thicker geometry, screws, or a stiffer material. These proposed fixes still needed testing."],"figures":[{"src":"assets/projects/airport-bridge-joint.png","alt":"CAD view of the bridge split into two sections with a slotting joint.","caption":"The two-piece bridge joint introduced to fit the printer’s build area."}]},{"id":"reflection","label":"Lessons","title":"Design for testing, then make time to test","paragraphs":["The software integration succeeded, while the final mechanism did not satisfy every requirement. That result made the value of early calculations, design reviews, and a record of failed ideas much clearer to me."],"bullets":["Challenge each concept as a team and check loads before committing to fabrication.","Keep an error log so later revisions do not repeat earlier failures.","Model and estimate print times earlier to preserve enough time for physical testing."]}],"number":"09"}]};
